{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 19, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.3-70b-instruct", "content": "the social network is a film that tells the story of the founding of facebook and the subsequent lawsuits that followed. the movie is directed by david fincher and written by aaron sorkin, who adapted the screenplay from the book \"the accidental billionaires\" by ben mezrich.\n\nthe film starts with mark zuckerberg, played by jesse eisenberg, as a harvard undergraduate who is trying to find his place in the world. he is a brilliant computer programmer, but he is also socially awkward and struggles to connect with people. after a painful breakup with his girlfriend, erica albright, played by rooney mara, mark creates a website called \"facemash\" that allows users to compare the photos of two students and vote on which one is more attractive.\n\nthe site becomes a huge success, but it also gets mark into trouble with the university administration. he is then approached by twin brothers cameron and tyler winklevoss, played by armie hammer, who want him to create a social networking site exclusively for harvard students. mark agrees to work on the project, but he soon drops the twins and starts working on his own version of the site, which he calls \"thefacebook\".\n\nas the site gains popularity, mark becomes increasingly obsessed with it, and his relationships with his friends and family begin to suffer. he is particularly close to his friend eduardo saverin, played by andrew garfield, who helps him to launch the site and provides financial backing. however, as the site grows, mark's relationship with eduardo begins to deteriorate, and they eventually have a falling out.\n\nthe film also explores the legal battles that mark faces as a result of the site's success. the winklevoss twins sue him for stealing their idea, and eduardo sues him for diluting his shares in the company. the film ends with mark settling the lawsuits and becoming one of the youngest billionaires in the world.\n\noverall, the social network is a fascinating film that explores the complexities of social networking and the consequences of success. the performances are excellent, with jesse eisenberg standing out as the brilliant but troubled mark zuckerberg. the film also features a great soundtrack, with a score by trent reznor and atticus ross that adds to the tension and drama of the story.\n\none of the things that makes the social network so compelling is its portrayal of the darker side of success. mark zuckerberg is a complex character who is both brilliant and flawed, and the film does not shy away from showing his weaknesses and vulnerabilities. the film also raises important questions about the nature of friendship and loyalty, and the ways in which success can change people and damage relationships.\n\nin terms of its historical accuracy, the social network has been the subject of some controversy. some of the characters, including mark zuckerberg, have disputed the film's portrayal of events, and some of the details have been fictionalized for dramatic effect. however, the film is generally considered to be a accurate portrayal of the founding of facebook and the subsequent lawsuits.\n\noverall, the social network is a gripping and thought-provoking film that explores the complexities of social networking and the consequences of success. it features excellent performances, a great soundtrack, and a compelling storyline that raises important questions about the nature of friendship and loyalty.", "logprobs": [{"token": "the", "logprob": -0.0032289293594658375}, {"token": " social", "logprob": -0.014163684099912643}, {"token": " network", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -0.006730150431394577}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": " film", "logprob": -0.21165671944618225}, {"token": " that", "logprob": -0.0006001578294672072}, {"token": " tells", "logprob": -0.2985791563987732}, {"token": " the"